WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.600
The cost function of the neural style transfer algorithm had a content cost component and a style cost component.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.200
Let's start by defining the content cost component.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.400
Remember that this is the overall cost function of the neural style transfer algorithm.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.600
So let's figure out what should the content cost function be.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.200
Let's say that you use hidden layer L to compute the content cost.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:37.800
If L is a very small number, if you use hidden layer 1, then it will really force your generated image to have pixel values very similar to your content image.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:45.800
Whereas if you use a very deep layer, then it's just asking, well, if there's a dog in your content image, then make sure there's a dog somewhere in your generated image.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.200
So in practice, the layer L chosen is somewhere in between.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.800
It's neither too shallow nor too deep in the neural network.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:04.600
And because you play with this yourself in the programming exercise that ended this week, I'll leave you to gain some intuitions with the concrete examples in the programming exercise as well.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:12.300
But usually L is chosen to be somewhere in the middle of the layers of the neural network, neither too shallow nor too deep.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:20.000
What you can do is then use a pre-trained continent, maybe a VGG network or it could be some other neural network as well.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:29.000
And now you want to measure, given a content image and given a generated image, how similar are they in content?

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:41.800
So let's let this A, super, super square bracket L, round bracket C, and this be the activations of layer L on these two images, on the images C and G.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:51.700
So if these two activations are similar, then that would seem to imply that both images have similar content.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:05.100
So what we'll do is define J content C, G as just how different, how similar, how different are these two activations.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:19.900
So we'll take the element-wise difference between these hidden unit activations in layer L, between when you pass in the content image compared to when you pass in the generated image and take that squared.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:26.500
And you could have a normalization constant in front or not, so just one of the two or something else, it doesn't really matter.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.300
Since this will be, can be adjusted as well by this hyperparameter alpha.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:42.520
So just be clear, I'm using this notation as if both of these have been unrolled into vectors.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:51.020
So then this becomes the squared of the L2 norm between this and this, after you've unrolled down both into vectors.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:58.120
But it's really just the element-wise sum of squared differences between these two activation.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:08.920
But it's really just the element-wise sum of squares of differences between the activations in layer L, between the images C and G.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:18.920
And so when later you perform gradient descent on J of G to try to find a value of G so that the overall cost is low,

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:27.920
this will incentivize the algorithm to find an image G so that these hidden layer activations are similar to what you got for the content.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.920
So that's how you define the content cost function for neural style transfer.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.920
Next, let's move on to the style cost function.
